WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.150
<v Instructor>Now let's talk about Cross-Zone Balancing.</v>

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:06.050
So let's take an example of a very imbalance situation

3
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.120
but it will illustrate the point.

4
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.130
So we have two Availability Zones

5
00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:11.840
and the first one has a load balancer

6
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.167
with two EC2 instances

7
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.530
and the second one also has a load balancer.

8
00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.050
So, a load balancer instance, obviously,

9
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.760
with eight EC2 instances.

10
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.680
The load balancers instances are part of the same,

11
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.090
more general load balancer.

12
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:28.960
So, the client is accessing these load balancers.

13
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.290
And so with Cross-Zone Load Balancing,

14
00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.520
each load balancer instance will distribute evenly

15
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.290
across all registered instances in all Availability Zones.

16
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.430
So the client itself is sending 50%

17
00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.700
of the traffic to the first ALB instance

18
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.080
and 50% of traffic to the other ALB instance.

19
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.590
But each ALB is going to redirect that traffic

20
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.310
across all 10 EC2 instances

21
00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:58.440
regardless of which Availability Zones they're in.

22
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.150
This is why it's called, Cross-Zone Load Balancing.

23
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.380
So, if we take a look at the first,

24
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.483
at the second ALB, sorry, instance

25
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:09.990
it's going to send 10% of the traffic it receives, okay,

26
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.700
to all these instances

27
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.120
because we have 10 instances,

28
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.900
so each of them gets 10% of the traffic.

29
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.500
And same story with this first ALB instance

30
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.870
it's going to send also 10% traffic to all these instances.

31
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.640
So in this example, with Cross-Zone Balancing,

32
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.150
we are distributing the traffic evenly

33
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.200
across all EC2 instances.

34
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.140
And that may be something you want to have

35
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.310
or not, who knows,

36
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.730
but at least this behavior is available to you.

37
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.470
The second behavior that's available to you

38
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.460
is to not have Cross-Zone Balancing.

39
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.830
So we take the same example,

40
00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.930
but without Cross-Zone Balancing

41
00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:47.838
the requests,

42
00:01:47.838 --> 00:01:49.060
the requests, sorry,

43
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.680
are distributed in the instances of the node

44
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.950
of the Elastic Load Balancer.

45
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.450
So this example, what we have

46
00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.460
is that the clients sends 50% of the traffic

47
00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.560
to the first AZ and the 50% of traffic to the second AZ.

48
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.043
Okay. But the first ALB instance is going to

49
00:02:06.043 --> 00:02:09.800
send the traffic to only the EC2 instances in its region.

50
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.530
So that means that each EC2 instance in this,

51
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:13.830
sorry, Availability Zone

52
00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.380
is going to get 25% of the traffic overall.

53
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.100
Okay. So half and half.

54
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.700
And the one on the right hand side

55
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.800
will again divide up the traffic it receives

56
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.560
into the EC2 instances.

57
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.710
The averages are within its AZ.

58
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.174
So AZ2.

59
00:02:26.174 --> 00:02:29.700
So as we can see here without Cross Load Balancing

60
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.180
the traffic is contained within each AZ.

61
00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.290
But if there are an imbalanced number of EC2 instances

62
00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.380
in each AZ, then some EC2 instances

63
00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.730
of a specific AZ will receive more traffic than others.

64
00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.730
It's just an option to know about.

65
00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.070
There's no right or wrong answer.

66
00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.760
It depends on the use case, obviously.

67
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.200
So let's talk about Cross-Zone Load Balancing

68
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.340
for each load balancer.

69
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.980
So for the CLB, it's disabled by default

70
00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.840
and you have no charges for inter AZ data, if enabled.

71
00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.450
For the Application Load Balancer,

72
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.520
it's always on, it cannot be disabled

73
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.750
and you have no charges

74
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.760
for inter AZ data as well.

75
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.830
For the Network Load Balancer

76
00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:07.920
is disabled by default

77
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.550
are you going to pay charges if you do enable this feature.

78
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.470
And finally for the Gateway Load Balancer

79
00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.550
it's disabled by default

80
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.260
and you are going to pay charges for inter AZ data

81
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.950
if you enable this feature.

82
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.530
Let's talk about Sticky Sessions,

83
00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.230
also called, Session Affinity,

84
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.000
for your Elastic Load Balancer.

85
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.470
So it's possible to implement what's called

86
00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.250
stickiness or sticky sessions.

87
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.280
And the idea is that the client doing two requests

88
00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.290
to the load balancer will have the same instance

89
00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.410
in the backend to respond to the requests.

90
00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:39.450
So the idea is that for example,

91
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.000
you have a ALB with two EC2 instances

92
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.430
and you have three clients,

93
00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:45.160
if the client one makes a request

94
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.430
and it goes to the first EC2 instance,

95
00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.890
that means that when it does a second request

96
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.723
to the load balancer,

97
00:03:50.723 --> 00:03:53.200
it will go to the same instance.

98
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.760
Which is a different behavior

99
00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.010
where usually the Application Load Balancer

100
00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.780
will do a spread of all the requests

101
00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.090
across all the EC2 instances.

102
00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:03.280
Now for client two,

103
00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:05.820
if it goes to the ALB and talks to the second instance,

104
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.610
then all the requests will go there.

105
00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.640
And same for client three, okay.

106
00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.420
So this is a behavior that can be enabled

107
00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.100
for the Classic Load Balancer

108
00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.340
and the Application Load Balancer.

109
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.190
And how it works,

110
00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.450
well there is a cookie that is sent as part of the request

111
00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.710
from the clients to the load balancer

112
00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.090
and has stickiness

113
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.640
and it has an expiration date.

114
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.280
That means that when the cookie expires,

115
00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.020
then the clients may be redirected

116
00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.270
to another EC2 instance.

117
00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.200
The use case for this

118
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.770
is to make sure that the user

119
00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.386
is connected to the same backend instance,

120
00:04:34.386 --> 00:04:37.470
in order not to lose his session data.

121
00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.290
Which can take some important information

122
00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:42.060
such as the login of the user, for example.

123
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.420
But if you enable stickiness,

124
00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.220
it may bring imbalance to the load

125
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.630
over the backend, EC2 instances.

126
00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:47.957
In case some instances,

127
00:04:47.957 --> 00:04:50.740
I have a very, very sticky user.

128
00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.060
Now let's have a look at

129
00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.340
Routing Algorithms for your requests.

130
00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.260
So, there is one called the Least Outstanding Request

131
00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.830
which is at the next instance to receive the request

132
00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.930
is the instance that has the lowest number of pending

133
00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.390
or unfinished requests.

134
00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:06.430
So basically the instance that is

135
00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:08.030
the less currently busy

136
00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:10.880
is the instance that will receive the next request.

137
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:11.740
Which makes sense, right?

138
00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.150
Because you want to make sure that

139
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.823
if an instance has less requests than another,

140
00:05:14.823 --> 00:05:17.740
then maybe has more capacity to receive these requests.

141
00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.823
So it's available for the ALB and the CLB.

142
00:05:19.823 --> 00:05:22.650
And so the idea is that the first request will go

143
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.070
maybe to the first instance.

144
00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.700
The second request to the second.

145
00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.952
And then the third request has to go to the third

146
00:05:27.952 --> 00:05:30.080
because the third instance right now,

147
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:31.500
doesn't have any requests.

148
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.880
So it has the Least Outstanding Requests.

149
00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.020
So this is how it works.

150
00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.660
And then the fourth one may go again

151
00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:38.960
to the third instance.

152
00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.770
If the third instance has the Least Outstanding Requests

153
00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:45.310
maybe the third request was very, very quick to finish

154
00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.160
and so the fourth one went again

155
00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.670
to the third instance and so on. Okay.

156
00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.250
Now, for the Round Robin, this is a bit different.

157
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:56.000
So the Round Robin is that the targets will have

158
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.390
one after the other, the next requests,

159
00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:01.260
regardless of how many outstanding requests are available

160
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.093
on your instance.

161
00:06:02.093 --> 00:06:04.270
And so this works for ALB and CLB.

162
00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.950
The idea is that number one will go to number one,

163
00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:06.870
number two will go to number two,

164
00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.320
and number three go to number one.

165
00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:09.410
And then number four,

166
00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.420
will again go through the same cycle.

167
00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:14.370
So number one and so on.

168
00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:15.203
Then for the NLB

169
00:06:15.203 --> 00:06:18.010
there is the Flow Hash Request Routing.

170
00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.330
Which a target is selected based on

171
00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.210
a hashing of the protocol,

172
00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.920
source/destination IP address,

173
00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.340
source/destination port,

174
00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.210
and TCP sequence number.

175
00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.920
That means that each TCP or UDP connection

176
00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:31.070
is going to be routed

177
00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.510
for a single target for the life of the connection

178
00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.070
which is sort of equivalent

179
00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:39.040
for the sticky sessions on the Network Load Balancer.

180
00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.490
So very, very simply

181
00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.460
whenever a user makes a request in EC2 instance

182
00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:44.950
all the information I just said below

183
00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.520
is going to be hashed,

184
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.340
through a Flow Hash Algorithm.

185
00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.010
And then thanks to this hash number

186
00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.080
is going to route the same requests

187
00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.710
from the same user to the same EC2 instance

188
00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.340
as long as the TCP connection is open, okay.

189
00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.800
And this is a specificity of your NLB.

190
00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.000
So that's it for this lecture.

191
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:02.833
I hope you liked it,

192
00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:04.720
and I will see you in the next lecture.

